\b LLM TCP Replication: Current Issues and Evidence Plan\b0\par
\par
\b Goal\b0\par
This project is intended to replicate the TCP-CI/Yaraghi evaluation setting while replacing the original RF/RankLib ranking model with a multi-agent LLM pipeline. The closest replication-style run uses the latest 50 failed builds, the 150-feature TCP-CI feature set, no candidate cap, hidden target Verdict and Duration, and APFD/APFDc evaluation after ranking.\par
\par
\b Main Current Issue: Runtime Without Candidate Cap\b0\par
The biggest practical issue is speed. With candidate cap disabled, every target build sends the full target test suite through the LLM pipeline. The paper's RF ranking model can score a build quickly after training, but the LLM approach requires multiple model calls per build.\par
\par
For each target build, the current LLM pipeline may perform:\par
1. Filter Agent calls over batches of all target-build tests.\par
2. Ranking Agent calls over batches of high-risk tests.\par
3. Optional Merge Agent call to globally reorder high-risk ranking batches.\par
\par
This means the cost grows with the number of target builds, number of tests per build, filter batch size, ranking batch size, and merge-agent usage. A full 25-dataset, 50-failed-build, no-cap run can be very slow on a local model such as qwen2.5:32b.\par
\par
\b What Is Now More Paper-Aligned\b0\par
The runner now gives the LLM target-build feature rows, with Verdict and Duration removed. This matches the paper's prediction setup more closely: rank tests for the target build using target-build features, then evaluate later using target Verdict and Duration. DET_COV_C_Faults and DET_COV_IMP_Faults are included as historical previously-detected-fault features from the 150-feature TCP-CI setting.\par
\par
\b What Still Differs From The Paper\b0\par
The paper trains RF/RankLib models on prior failed builds. This project replaces that learned model with a multi-agent LLM process. The Filter Agent's T1-T6 split and deterministic T6 tail are part of the LLM method, not part of the original RF method. Therefore the correct claim is replication of the TCP-CI feature/evaluation setting with an LLM ranking-method substitution, not exact method replication.\par
\par
\b Evidence CSV For Professor\b0\par
The runner now supports --llm-trace-csv. For a one-build run, this CSV appends one row per test. Each row records:\par
- Dataset and target build.\par
- Sanitized feature JSON shown to the LLM.\par
- Flags proving Verdict and Duration were not shown to the LLM.\par
- Final LLM priority, confidence, and reason.\par
- Actual Verdict and Duration used only for evaluation.\par
- APFD, APFDc, Recall@10, and build wall time.\par
\par
\b Suggested One-Build Evidence Command\b0\par
python3 scripts/run_llm_agent.py \\\par
  --data datasets/apache@rocketmq.csv \\\par
  --results-csv results/professor_one_build_summary.csv \\\par
  --llm-trace-csv results/professor_one_build_trace.csv \\\par
  --filter-model qwen2.5:32b \\\par
  --ranking-model qwen2.5:32b \\\par
  --openai-base-url http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1 \\\par
  --eval-window 1 \\\par
  --history-failed-builds-only \\\par
  --candidate-cap 0 \\\par
  --batch-size 10 \\\par
  --ranking-batch-size 12 \\\par
  --ranking-workers 2 \\\par
  --quiet\par
\par
\b Recommendation\b0\par
Use the no-cap one-build trace as evidence of correctness and transparency. For full evaluation, expect significant runtime. If runtime is too high, candidate-cap experiments can still be reported, but they should be described as capped LLM experiments rather than exact paper-style full-suite runs.\par
